.TH HOST 1
.SH NAME
host, nslookup \- resolve an IPv4 hostname or reverse address
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B host
.RI NAME|ADDRESS
.br
.B nslookup
.RI NAME|ADDRESS
.SH DESCRIPTION
Print IPv4 addresses for NAME, or look up the PTR name of ADDRESS.
Nslookup is an alias for this small, noninteractive host tool.
Local aliases in /etc/hosts are checked before DNS. Nameservers come from
/etc/resolv.conf, with up to three numeric IPv4 nameserver entries.
The installed defaults are 1.1.1.1 and 8.8.8.8; configuration survives upgrades.
.PP
DNS follows bounded CNAME chains and retries truncated UDP replies over TCP.
Up to four IPv4 addresses are returned. Queries use monotonic timeouts and
do not replace the caller's alarm or signal handlers. Unknown names and
missing address records return a nonzero status. Search domains, IPv6 host
results and a DNS cache are not supplied. Use dig for other record types.
.SH SEE ALSO
dig(1), ping(1), telnet(1)
